<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">1992 Summer Olympics</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Barcelona 1992</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1992 Summer Olympics were held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, from 25 July to 9 August 1992. The first Games after the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> without a major boycott, they included a Unified Team of former Soviet republics and a reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> returned after decades of exclusion. The preparations transformed the city's waterfront and transport, and became a model for urban renewal through the Olympics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1992-summer-olympics.jpg" alt="1992 Summer Olympics" width="330" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikolai Karaneschev · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barcelona_AUGUST_1992_the_Olympic_Games_(Juegos_Olímpicos_de_Barcelona_1992)_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the <b>1992 Summer Olympics</b> in Barcelona the South African team used &quot;Ode to Joy&quot; and a neutral flag instead.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-stem-van-suid-afrika">Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1992 <b>Barcelona Olympics</b> and Seville Expo marked modernisation.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-spain">Kingdom of Spain</a></li></ul></section>
